crossover-wheel, macOS arm64.

Force feedback for a Thrustmaster T150 in games running under CrossOver.

No kext, no system extension, no SIP or AMFI change, and no password at any
point. Everything happens in your own account and the daemon that talks to the
wheel runs unprivileged.

INSTALL

Open the disk image and drag crossover-wheel onto the Applications shortcut
beside it. Then launch it from Applications.

The first launch only: RIGHT-CLICK the app and choose Open, rather than
double-clicking, and confirm. macOS marks everything a browser downloads and
asks about anything not signed by a paid Apple developer account. Nobody has
paid for one here. You meet that dialog once and never again.

No password is asked for at any point. Nothing is installed outside your own
account.

The app then asks which CrossOver bottle your game is in and puts the proxy
there. That is the only thing it installs: it carries its own copy of the
daemon and runs that.

IF IT SAYS IT WAS PREVENTED FROM MODIFYING APPS

Turn on crossover-wheel in System Settings, Privacy and Security, App
Management, then press Install again.

It does not modify any application. What it needs is to READ one file that
belongs to CrossOver, its own copy of dinput8.dll, which lives inside
CrossOver.app, and macOS puts reaching inside another app's bundle behind that
switch whether you are reading or writing.

Afterwards it lives in your menu bar as a small steering wheel:

  thin centre    nothing running, or no wheel plugged in
  filled centre  the daemon has the wheel
  full centre    a game is pulling on it right now

Start and stop the daemon there, and switch on "Start at login" if you would
rather never think about it.

"Check for updates..." in that menu installs new versions by itself: it
downloads, checks the file against its published checksum, replaces the app
and starts it again. You do not have to drag anything or answer any macOS
dialog for an update, only for the very first install.

If you make another CrossOver bottle later, "Install into a bottle..." in that
menu adds the proxy to it. That is the only thing the app ever installs: it
carries its own copy of the daemon and runs that, so nothing has to be put on
your PATH for the menu bar to work.

The command line tools, t150ctl and t150boot and the probes, are optional and
are not installed by the app. ./install.sh from the source tree is where they
come from if you want them.

THEN, EVERY TIME

If you use the app, none of this is needed: start the daemon from the menu
bar, or switch on "Start at login" and forget it exists.

By hand:

  t150boot            take the wheel out of boot mode. Needed after every
                      plug-in, and after sleep and wake. The daemon does this
                      by itself while it is running, so it is only needed
                      before you start the daemon.
  t150d -v -w         start this before the game, and leave it running.

The proxy looks for the daemon once, when the game creates its wheel, and
never again, so the order matters. -w writes to the wheel from a separate
thread, which is what stops a game reporting 100% CPU; it is not the default
only because it is newer than the rest.

SET YOUR GAME TO 1080 DEGREES

Nothing can tell a wheel what rotation range to be at: on Windows that number
lives in Thrustmaster's control panel, and a game simply assumes the wheel is
already at whatever the game's own setting says. This wheel is 1080 and games
often default to 900, so out of the box they disagree and the car responds to
your hands by 900/1080 of what it means to.

Set the game to 1080 and they agree. t150ctl range moves the wheel instead,
if you would rather drive at a shorter range: set both to the same number.

TWO THINGS macOS WILL DO TO YOU

Sit at the machine. Writing to the wheel is gated on being the console user,
so this fails over SSH, from the login window and from a fast-user-switched
session.

Approve the wheel. On an Apple Silicon laptop: System Settings, Privacy and
Security, Accessories. Until you do, the wheel appears nowhere at all.

These binaries are unsigned and not notarized, so a browser download is
quarantined and macOS refuses to run it. install.sh clears that flag on
everything it installs.

IF THE WHEEL EVER MOVES ON ITS OWN

Start and stop t150d. It stops every effect and closes the wheel's input the
moment it takes the wheel, because that state outlives whatever set it, so a
wheel left rendering something by an earlier program would otherwise carry on.
A replug also lets it re-find its centre.

WHAT IS NOT PERFECT

With a condition effect running, the wheel gives a small knock when it is left
standing at 0, 135, 270 and 405 degrees. That is the wheel's own damper loop
and not this software: it reproduces with one raw packet and no game, no
daemon and no proxy running. The daemon already holds the coefficient below
the value where it becomes a continuous buzz.

Springs, envelopes, ramps and per-effect gain are encoded to match
Thrustmaster's own Windows driver byte for byte, and no one has felt them on a
wheel yet. A constant force, a damper and two periodics have been.

The man pages are here. Read one with:

  man ./t150d.8

docs/RESEARCH.md in the repository is the evidence behind all of this.
